\begin{proof}
Suppose $H$ and $K$ are normal subgroups of $G$. Since the intersection of two subgroups is a subgroup, $H\cap K$ is a subgroup of $G$. Let $g\in G$ and $x\in H\cap K$. Then $x\in H$ and $x\in K$. Normality of $H$ and $K$ gives $gxg^{-1}\in H$ and $gxg^{-1}\in K$, so $gxg^{-1}\in H\cap K$. Thus $g(H\cap K)g^{-1}\subseteq H\cap K$. Applying the same inclusion with $g^{-1}$ in place of $g$ yields the reverse inclusion, so $g(H\cap K)g^{-1}=H\cap K$ for every $g\in G$. Therefore $H\cap K\unlhd G$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $H$ and $K$ are normal subgroups of $G$. Since the intersection of two subgroups is a subgroup, $H\cap K$ is a subgroup of $G$. For any $g\in G$ and $x\in H\cap K$, we have $x\in H$ and $x\in K$. Because $H$ and $K$ are normal in $G$, it follows that $gxg^{-1}\in H$ and $gxg^{-1}\in K$. Hence $gxg^{-1}\in H\cap K$, and therefore

a) $g(H\cap K)g^{-1}\subseteq H\cap K$.

Replacing $g$ by $g^{-1}$ in this inclusion and conjugating by $g$ gives $H\cap K\subseteq g(H\cap K)g^{-1}$. Thus equality holds for every $g\in G$, so $H\cap K\unlhd G$.
\end{proof}
